JavaScript's `Array.prototype.filter()` method creates a new array containing all elements from the original array that pass a test implemented by a provided callback function. It does not mutate the original array — it returns a new, filtered array. The callback receives three arguments: the current element, its index, and the original array. The callback should return `true` to keep the element or `false` to exclude it. The basic syntax is: `const newArray = array.filter(callback(element[, index[, array]])[, thisArg])`. Here is a simple example filtering numbers greater than 10:

```javascript
const numbers = [5, 12, 8, 130, 44];
const filtered = numbers.filter(num => num > 10);
console.log(filtered); // [12, 130, 44]
```

When filtering arrays of objects, you access object properties inside the callback. For example, filtering users who are active:

```javascript
const users = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Alice', active: true },
  { id: 2, name: 'Bob', active: false },
  { id: 3, name: 'Charlie', active: true }
];
const activeUsers = users.filter(user => user.active);
console.log(activeUsers);
// [{ id: 1, name: 'Alice', active: true }, { id: 3, name: 'Charlie', active: true }]
```

You can combine multiple conditions with logical operators. For example, filtering products that are in stock and cost less than $50:

```javascript
const products = [
  { name: 'Laptop', price: 999, inStock: true },
  { name: 'Mouse', price: 25, inStock: true },
  { name: 'Keyboard', price: 75, inStock: false },
  { name: 'USB Cable', price: 10, inStock: true }
];
const affordable = products.filter(p => p.inStock && p.price < 50);
console.log(affordable);
// [{ name: 'Mouse', price: 25, inStock: true }, { name: 'USB Cable', price: 10, inStock: true }]
```

You can also use the index parameter to filter by position, for instance to get every other element:

```javascript
const everyOther = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'].filter((_, i) => i % 2 === 0);
console.log(everyOther); // ['a', 'c', 'e']
```

A common pattern is chaining filter with other array methods like map and reduce. For example, get the names of all users over 18:

```javascript
const people = [
  { name: 'John', age: 20 },
  { name: 'Jane', age: 17 },
  { name: 'Jim', age: 25 }
];
const adultNames = people.filter(p => p.age >= 18).map(p => p.name);
console.log(adultNames); // ['John', 'Jim']
```

For filtering out falsy values from an array, you can pass the Boolean constructor:

```javascript
const mixed = [0, 'hello', false, null, 42, '', undefined];
const truthy = mixed.filter(Boolean);
console.log(truthy); // ['hello', 42]
```

Note that `filter()` is available on all arrays in modern JavaScript and IE9+. It is a handy, declarative alternative to writing explicit for-loops with conditional checks. Because it returns a new array and avoids side effects, it aligns well with functional programming principles and makes code more readable and maintainable.
